{
  // Every fence in this repo lives in THIS file and nowhere else. oxlint resolves the nearest
  // config, so a second .oxlintrc.json anywhere below the root would replace this one for its whole
  // subtree and leave `pnpm lint` green. The three oxlint scripts therefore pin
  // `--config .oxlintrc.json`, which makes a nested config inert, and `pnpm check:oxlint-config`
  // reports both a stray config and a script that lost the pin. Narrow a rule with an "overrides"
  // entry below, never with a nested config file — and never with a .gitignore line either. oxlint
  // and oxfmt both walk the tree honoring gitignore at any depth, so a gitignore line over a
  // tracked source file removes it from linting AND formatting while the file still ships; the pin
  // does not reach that, so `pnpm check:oxlint-config` reports it by name and ignore line. An "overrides"
  // entry REPLACES a rule's options wholesale, so every scoped fence below is a verbatim COPY of the root
  // list: restate each root group byte-identically (group array AND message), or exempt this scope from one
  // with an `oxlint-omission` marker line beside that entry's own rule key — the marker names one specifier
  // of the omitted group and then a reason, and the two below are the pattern. `check:oxlint-config` asserts
  // both directions, including a marker that has gone stale or vacuous. Do not spell a marker in prose up
  // here: this header is the ROOT site's own region, and the root may not exempt itself from its own group.
  // `plugins` REPLACES oxlint's default set, so it restates that set (eslint, typescript, unicorn,
  // oxc) and then adds the plugins this file opts in to. A plugin dropped from this list turns
  // every rule below that it owns into a silent no-op.
  "plugins": ["eslint", "typescript", "unicorn", "oxc", "react", "import"],
  "jsPlugins": [
    "oxlint-tailwindcss",
    "./scripts/oxlint/anti-slop/index.ts",
    "./scripts/oxlint/alfred/index.ts"
  ],
  "categories": {},
  "options": {
    "typeAware": true
  },
  "rules": {
    // The vendored anti-slop rules (scripts/oxlint/anti-slop/, see its README for the
    // upstream commits and for the upstream rules this repo does not vendor).
    //
    // Eleven rules at `error`. Each was either adopted clean (zero violations on arrival,
    // so it can only fire on code written after adoption) or driven to zero at warn and
    // promoted here. That is also the bar for registering a new rule — a rule the tree
    // already violates belongs in its own change, violations first, rule second.
    // require-readable-spacing is the one autofix rule: adopted by running its
    // whitespace-only `--fix` across the tree to zero, so the diff added blank lines
    // and nothing else.
    // Four rules were promoted after a by-surface scoping: product source was
    // driven to zero and the test/eval scopes, the two ops-scripts trees
    // (apps/server/src/scripts, packages/*/src/scripts), plus named
    // honest-boundary files where the flagged spelling IS the boundary parse,
    // are exempted by "overrides" entries at the bottom of this file. Repo-level
    // scripts/ gates are NOT exempt — each carries its own per-site disable. See
    // docs/research/anti-slop-paydown-2026-09-12.md for the promotion bar.
    // (no-unsafe-dictionary-type, no-unknown-returns, and no-runtime-typeof.)
    "anti-slop/no-module-mocking": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-reflect-apply": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-widen-then-assert": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-object-parameters": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-chained-type-assertions": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-known-value-widening": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-unknown-type-aliases": "error",
    "anti-slop/require-readable-spacing": "error",
    // No rule remains at warn for paydown. Three upstream rules stay out:
    // - no-conditional-empty-object-spread: conflicts with exactOptionalPropertyTypes
    // - no-unknown-parameters: conflicts with boundary validator pattern
    // - no-reflect-get: conflicts with Reflect.get for class instances
    // no-shape-in-symbol-names was dropped in #1149: a substring ban cannot tell
    // the credential `shape` field from the DayShape domain, so it is documented
    // in docs/reference/review-checklist.md instead of enforced.
    // allowInTypeGuards: `typeof` inside a `value is T` predicate is the predicate's
    // correct implementation, not the ad-hoc narrowing this rule exists to reject.
    "anti-slop/no-runtime-typeof": ["error", { "allowInTypeGuards": true }],
    "anti-slop/no-unknown-returns": "error",
    "anti-slop/no-unsafe-dictionary-type": "error",
    // Alfred's own rules (scripts/oxlint/alfred/, first-party, see its README). All but one
    // were regex gates in scripts/consolidation-rules.mjs that needed scope analysis to stop
    // missing cases and flagging look-alikes; no-exported-hasher was born here. Each keeps the
    // gate's file scope by itself (`inGateScope` reads `isSkippedPath`), and a rule that had
    // per-rule `owners` keeps them as a closed list in its own source, so none needs an
    // "overrides" entry here.
    // no-function-wrapper-shim: an exported function that only returns a constant or an
    // imported name. sha256-over-json-stringify: a hash digest over JSON.stringify output,
    // whose pre-image depends on key order; use sha256Canonical. no-exported-hasher: a
    // function outside @alfred/db/hash that is exported and hashes its parameter, which
    // keeps the set of cross-file hashers closed for the sha256 rule.
    // no-constants-re-export / no-type-re-export-shim: a logic file that hands out a value /
    // a type of a `constants` module again; only the package barrel may. no-type-alias-shim:
    // an exported type that is only a new name for an imported type.
    // no-union-enum-without-default: a `t.UnionEnum` call with no `default` key, which
    // would fill a missing field with the first member.
    // hand-rolled-key-guard: a hand-written key test of a closed table (`Object.hasOwn`,
    // `x in TABLE`, `TABLE[x] === undefined`); the last two are true for an inherited key
    // such as "toString". Use keyGuard(TABLE).
    // ai-call-needs-abort-signal: an AI SDK generate or stream call (bare or `metered*`) whose
    // options show no `abortSignal` key. The wrappers' 600 s `timeout` is a backstop, not a
    // cancel path, so a caller that stops waiting keeps a billable call open (#1086, #714).
    // A pass-through wrapper whose options the rule cannot read takes a disable with its reason.
    "alfred/ai-call-needs-abort-signal": "error",
    "alfred/hand-rolled-key-guard": "error",
    // log-caught-error-under-err: a caught error, or its message, in a `logger` call anywhere
    // but as the raw value under `err`. Only `err` goes through the redacting serializer.
    // `safeErrorDiagnostic(err)` is the safe text form under another key.
    "alfred/log-caught-error-under-err": "error",
    // no-never-sentinel-binding: a `const x: never` in a `switch` clause. `never` is assignable
    // to every type, so `return x`, `Boolean(x)` or `{ text: x }` compiles, and a runtime value
    // that reaches the clause leaves as a valid result (#1566, #1547, #1219). Close the switch
    // with `assertNever(value, what)`, or return a real value of the return type.
    "alfred/no-never-sentinel-binding": "error",
    // required-executor-param: an optional or defaulted parameter or property whose type names
    // `DbTransaction`, or a union of it with `undefined`. A caller that omits it still compiles,
    // and the write runs on a second pooled connection, outside its transaction and locks
    // (#1526, #1225, #1224, #1114). Take `exec: DbTransaction | "own"`, or a plain
    // `DbTransaction` when each caller holds one.
    "alfred/required-executor-param": "error",
    // no-swallowed-catch: a `.catch(…)` handler that drops the error and returns a constant, such
    // as `.catch(() => null)`. The fault looks like a real result and no log shows it (#1458,
    // #1122). Log it and keep the value, return a failure value the caller tells apart, or keep
    // an expected swallow (autoplay, best-effort close) with a disable comment and its reason.
    "alfred/no-swallowed-catch": "error",
    // switch-literal-needs-default: a `switch` whose `case` tests are all string literals and
    // that has no `default`. switch-exhaustiveness-check proves each union member has a case, but
    // a stored row or a peer at deploy skew can hold a member this build does not know, and it
    // falls through (#1535, #1132, #1103). The `default` calls `assertNever(value, what)` when
    // the value was parsed at its boundary, or returns a real value when unparsed data reaches it.
    "alfred/switch-literal-needs-default": "error",
    // no-brand-mint-outside-owner: a type guard (`x is Brand`) or a `z.custom<Brand>()` of a
    // `unique symbol` brand outside the brand's owner file. Both mint the brand with no cast,
    // so a doc that says "only foo() mints it" was false (#1540, #1548). The rule keeps a
    // closed BRANDS table, and it reports a brand declaration that the table does not list.
    "alfred/no-brand-mint-outside-owner": "error",
    // The web motion and copy rules read one element's classes or JSX:
    // transition-names-individual-transform: a `transition-[…transform…]` list that misses the
    // `scale`/`translate`/`rotate` property a Tailwind v4 utility on the element writes.
    // no-delta-class-utility-collision: a `scale-*` utility on an `app-press` element without
    // `not-active:`, or a `translate-*` utility on an `app-hover-lift` element.
    // motion-safe-variant-animate: a variant `animate-*` without `motion-safe:` beside
    // `motion-reduce:animate-none`. no-conditional-motion-ref: a conditional `ref` on an
    // `m.*`/`motion.*` element. no-dash-in-web-copy: an em or en dash in web copy.
    "alfred/motion-safe-variant-animate": "error",
    "alfred/no-conditional-motion-ref": "error",
    "alfred/no-constants-re-export": "error",
    "alfred/no-dash-in-web-copy": "error",
    "alfred/no-delta-class-utility-collision": "error",
    "alfred/no-exported-hasher": "error",
    "alfred/no-function-wrapper-shim": "error",
    "alfred/no-type-alias-shim": "error",
    "alfred/no-type-re-export-shim": "error",
    "alfred/no-union-enum-without-default": "error",
    "alfred/sha256-over-json-stringify": "error",
    "alfred/transition-names-individual-transform": "error",
    // The tailwind rules sit at `error` by the same bar: zero violations on promotion, and
    // `pnpm lint` does not fail on `warn`, so a warn-level rule here enforced nothing.
    // `pnpm check:oxlint-plugin` drives only the anti-slop and alfred rules, not these.
    // enforce-canonical landed with the tree autofixed to zero; the built CSS kept every
    // declaration block. One directive keeps a `[&_…]:` variant group in one spelling.
    "tailwindcss/enforce-sort-order": ["error", { "entryPoint": "apps/web/src/index.css" }],
    "tailwindcss/enforce-shorthand": "error",
    "tailwindcss/no-deprecated-classes": ["error", { "entryPoint": "apps/web/src/index.css" }],
    "tailwindcss/enforce-canonical": "error",
    // Syntax rules from oxlint's built-in plugins, each adopted at zero by the same bar.
    // consistent-type-imports: an import used only as a type, including only inside `typeof`,
    // takes `type`. `verbatimModuleSyntax` already fails `tsc` on a type imported without it,
    // but a value imported only for `typeof` compiles and stays in the runtime import graph.
    // `fixStyle` matches the tree: a mixed import marks each type inline. The fixer then
    // writes `import { type X }` for a declaration whose every name is a type, and
    // no-import-type-side-effects below rewrites that as `import type { X }`.
    // `disallowTypeAnnotations` is off: an `import("…")` type is erased, and the
    // `test/type/*.type-test.ts` fixtures need it to probe the exports map.
    "typescript/consistent-type-imports": [
      "error",
      { "disallowTypeAnnotations": false, "fixStyle": "inline-type-imports" }
    ],
    // no-import-type-side-effects: `import { type X }` with every name a type keeps an empty
    // side-effect import (`import {} from "…"`) under `verbatimModuleSyntax`, which can run the
    // module or close an import cycle. Five review rounds found it before this rule (#1520,
    // #1554, #680, #669, #742). Adopted with the tree autofixed to zero.
    "typescript/no-import-type-side-effects": "error",
    // no-empty: an empty block, an empty `catch {}` included (`allowEmptyCatch` stays false). A
    // `catch` handles the failure, logs safe fields, or holds a comment that says why the failure
    // is safe to drop. A comment inside the block counts as content, so the reason is the fix.
    "no-empty": "error",
    // no-useless-spread: oxlint's default set has it at `warn`, which `pnpm lint` does not
    // fail on. Its common hit here is `{ ...(x ? { k: x } : {}) }` with nothing else in the
    // literal; pass the conditional itself (docs/reference/typescript.md).
    "unicorn/no-useless-spread": "error",
    // no-array-sort: `sort` reorders its input, so a sort of a Replicache subscription result,
    // a query cache entry, or a prop reorders it for every other reader (#1572, #1577). Use
    // `toSorted`. A bare `xs.sort(...)` statement stays legal (the default
    // `allowExpressionStatement`): it sorts a local array the code built for that purpose.
    // Adopted with the tree autofixed to zero.
    "unicorn/no-array-sort": "error",
    // React rules, from the `react` plugin in `plugins` above. rules-of-hooks: a hook called
    // conditionally, in a loop, or outside a component or hook. exhaustive-deps: a value that a
    // `useEffect` / `useCallback` / `useMemo` callback reads but its dependency array leaves out,
    // or a dependency the callback never reads. When the callback reads a local (`userId`), list
    // that local, not the expression it came from (`session?.user.id`): the rule matches names.
    "react/rules-of-hooks": "error",
    "react/exhaustive-deps": "error",
    // no-array-index-key: the `.map` index as a `key`. Key a row by a domain id, or by a value
    // derived from its content (a token's offset in its text, a tick's angle). A list that has no
    // id and never reorders (the ask_user questions) keeps the index with a per-site directive.
    "react/no-array-index-key": "error",
    // Enabling the plugin also arms its default `correctness` rules at `warn`, which `pnpm lint`
    // does not fail on. Each one is set explicitly here. These had zero hits when the plugin
    // landed, so they go straight to `error`, default options:
    "react/error-boundaries": "error",
    "react/forward-ref-uses-ref": "error",
    "react/globals": "error",
    "react/immutability": "error",
    "react/incompatible-library": "error",
    "react/jsx-key": "error",
    "react/jsx-no-duplicate-props": "error",
    "react/jsx-no-undef": "error",
    "react/jsx-props-no-spread-multi": "error",
    "react/no-children-prop": "error",
    "react/no-danger-with-children": "error",
    "react/no-did-mount-set-state": "error",
    "react/no-did-update-set-state": "error",
    "react/no-direct-mutation-state": "error",
    "react/no-find-dom-node": "error",
    "react/no-is-mounted": "error",
    "react/no-render-return-value": "error",
    "react/no-string-refs": "error",
    "react/no-this-in-sfc": "error",
    "react/no-unsafe": "error",
    "react/no-will-update-set-state": "error",
    "react/preserve-manual-memoization": "error",
    "react/set-state-in-render": "error",
    "react/static-components": "error",
    "react/use-memo": "error",
    "react/void-dom-elements-no-children": "error",
    "react/void-use-memo": "error",
    // refs: a `ref.current` read or write during render, driven to zero before it landed. Read
    // or write a ref in an effect or an event handler. A value that render reads belongs in state;
    // a mutable object that lives as long as the component is a lazy `useState(() => new Map())`.
    "react/refs": "error",
    // purity: an impure call (`Date.now()`, `Math.random()`) during render, driven to zero before
    // it landed. Read a clock in a lazy `useState` initializer, an effect or an event handler.
    "react/purity": "error",
    // set-state-in-effect: a `setState` called synchronously in an effect body, driven to zero
    // before it landed. Derive the value in render, adjust state in render when an input changes,
    // or tag a subscription snapshot with its inputs (`useReplicacheSubscription`).
    "react/set-state-in-effect": "error",
    // Type-aware rules. `options.typeAware` above runs them through oxlint-tsgolint (the tsgo
    // compiler, a root devDependency), so every oxlint invocation — `pnpm lint`, the edit-time
    // hook, the check:oxlint-config probe — reads types with no CLI flag to keep in step. A
    // missing tsgolint binary fails the run; it does not skip these rules.
    //
    // Four rules at `error`, each driven to zero before it landed here, by the same bar as the
    // groups above. no-floating-promises names the node:test registrars as known-safe calls:
    // `test(...)` and `describe(...)` return a promise the runner owns, and it is not awaited
    // on purpose. switch-exhaustiveness-check keeps its defaults: a `default` does NOT count as
    // the exhaustive case for a union, so a union switch names every member even when it has a
    // `default`. That is why the `default` needs no `never` binding: it calls `assertNever` or
    // returns a real value (allowDefaultCaseForExhaustiveSwitch stays true, which that default
    // needs). requireDefaultForNonUnion stays false: an open `string` such as
    // `event.key` has no member set to prove, so an empty `default` would add nothing. A switch
    // over the ESTree node union in scripts/oxlint/ carries its own directive with the reason.
    // no-misused-promises and restrict-template-expressions keep their defaults.
    //
    // Turning type-aware on also arms every type-aware rule in oxlint's default `correctness`
    // set at `warn`, which `pnpm lint` does not fail on. Each one is set explicitly below at
    // `error`, so the run reports only the rules this file chose. A new type-aware rule lands
    // the same way: tree to zero first, then the rule at `error`.
    "typescript/no-floating-promises": [
      "error",
      {
        "allowForKnownSafeCalls": [
          {
            "from": "package",
            "package": "node:test",
            "name": ["describe", "it", "suite", "test"]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "typescript/no-misused-promises": "error",
    "typescript/switch-exhaustiveness-check": "error",
    "typescript/restrict-template-expressions": "error",
    // only-throw-error: a `throw` of a value that is not an `Error`. A caught `unknown` may be
    // rethrown as it is (the rule's defaults). TanStack Router's `throw redirect()` and
    // `throw notFound()` are its control-flow API, so `allow` names those two return types.
    "typescript/only-throw-error": [
      "error",
      {
        "allow": [
          {
            "from": "package",
            "package": "@tanstack/router-core",
            "name": ["Redirect", "NotFoundError"]
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    // The no-unsafe-* family, each driven to zero before it landed: an `any` may not flow
    // into a return, an argument, a call, a member read, or an assignment. Type external,
    // persisted, and protocol data as `unknown` and parse it at the owning boundary
    // (CLAUDE.md) instead of letting `any` carry it. They are off for JavaScript sources
    // in the last "overrides" entry; JSDoc-typed `.mjs` is `any` by construction.
    "typescript/no-unsafe-return": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-argument": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-call": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-member-access": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-assignment": "error",
    // no-explicit-any closes the declaration half that the family above cannot see: an `any`
    // that is written but not yet read. Write `unknown` and narrow it, or name the type. For
    // "any zod schema" write `z.ZodType`, whose output defaults to `unknown`. It applies to
    // every TypeScript surface, tests included, with no override.
    "typescript/no-explicit-any": "error",
    // Six of the default `correctness` rules, adopted at zero (TypeScript and JavaScript
    // sources alike), default options: an `await` of a non-thenable, `delete` on an array
    // element, `for…in` over an array, a string passed to `setTimeout` / `new Function`,
    // unary minus on a non-number, and a default value for a binding that is never undefined.
    "typescript/await-thenable": "error",
    "typescript/no-array-delete": "error",
    "typescript/no-for-in-array": "error",
    "typescript/no-implied-eval": "error",
    "typescript/no-unsafe-unary-minus": "error",
    "typescript/no-useless-default-assignment": "error",
    // no-deprecated: a use of a symbol whose declaration carries `@deprecated`, from a
    // dependency (zod 4, the AI SDK, React types, TanStack, Sentry) or from this repo. Migrate
    // to the replacement the tag names; delete a repo-owned deprecated export once its last
    // caller moves. A site that must read the old symbol carries a directive with the reason.
    "typescript/no-deprecated": "error",
    // Default `correctness` rules that had hits, each driven to zero before it landed, default
    // options: `void` on an expression that is already `void`, a spread of a string (split a
    // string into code points with `Array.from`, which states the intent), a union or
    // intersection that repeats a member, and `.sort()` with no comparator. no-duplicate-type-
    // constituents also reports `| undefined` on an optional PARAMETER; an optional property
    // keeps it, because exactOptionalPropertyTypes is on. require-array-sort-compare keeps
    // `ignoreStringArrays` on (its default): a `string[]` sorts by UTF-16 code unit, which is
    // deterministic, and `localeCompare` would change that order. The rule targets numbers,
    // tuples and objects, which a bare `.sort()` orders by their string form. It applies to
    // `.mjs` too, where a JSDoc-untyped array is `any[]` and needs the comparator.
    "typescript/no-meaningless-void-operator": "error",
    "typescript/no-misused-spread": "error",
    "typescript/no-duplicate-type-constituents": "error",
    "typescript/require-array-sort-compare": "error",
    // return-await, correctness option only: a promise returned without `await` inside a `try`
    // (or a `catch` that has a `finally`) settles after the block exits, so its `catch` never sees
    // the rejection and its `finally` runs too early. The option reports only that case. The
    // default option also asks for style edits outside a `try`, and those change no behavior.
    "typescript/return-await": ["error", "error-handling-correctness-only"],
    // no-unnecessary-condition: a `?.`, `??`, truthiness check, or compare whose operand's type
    // already decides the result. Delete the guard only when a real parse established the type;
    // when the type is an assertion over unvalidated data (`jsonb.$type<X>()` rows that predate X,
    // `JSON.parse`, `res.json()`, a hand-typed frame), fix the type or parse at the boundary.
    // `while (true)` and `for (;;)` stay legal; a variable typed as the literal `true` does not.
    // It applies to every surface, tests and JavaScript included, with no override.
    "typescript/no-unnecessary-condition": [
      "error",
      { "allowConstantLoopConditions": "only-allowed-literals" }
    ],
    // no-non-null-assertion: a postfix `!`, a runtime promise the compiler cannot keep. Use the
    // value the check already produced (destructure, `.at(-1)`, iterate entries, a local that keeps
    // the narrowing); when absence is impossible, throw an `Error` that names the invariant; when it
    // is possible, handle it. Never trade `!` for `as T`. It applies to every surface, tests, evals,
    // scripts, and JavaScript included, with no override.
    "typescript/no-non-null-assertion": "error",
    // no-unnecessary-type-assertion: an `as T` or `<T>` that does not change the type, an `as T`
    // where the context already expects `T` and the value fits it, and a `!` on a value that is
    // never null or undefined. Delete the assertion; when it was the only place a reader learned
    // the type, annotate the binding or the return type instead. A `SAFETY:` comment that
    // explained the deleted cast goes with it. It applies to every surface, tests, evals,
    // scripts, and JavaScript included, with no override.
    "typescript/no-unnecessary-type-assertion": "error",
    // no-unsafe-type-assertion: an `as T` that narrows, so the compiler takes the claim on trust.
    // Fix it in this order: delete the cast; parse the value at its owning boundary with the zod
    // schema; narrow with a guard (`isRecord`, `getPath`, a `value is T` predicate); mint a brand
    // in the one function that owns it. For object keys and entries use `typedKeys`,
    // `typedEntries`, `recordFromKeys`, `pluckField`, and `hasEveryKey` from @alfred/contracts;
    // for a test fake use the package's `asTestDouble`. A cast that stays (a brand mint, a generic
    // walker, an SDK type gap) carries a per-site disable with its reason. It applies to every
    // surface, tests, evals, scripts, and JavaScript included, with no override.
    "typescript/no-unsafe-type-assertion": "error",
    // Three more default rules that had hits, each driven to zero before it landed:
    // - unbound-method: a method read as a value loses `this`. Bind it or wrap it in an arrow;
    //   declare a member that never reads `this` as a function-typed property.
    // - no-redundant-type-constituents: a union member another member absorbs (`unknown | X`,
    //   `"a" | string`). Write the wide type and say the rest in the doc comment, derive the
    //   exact union, or keep autocomplete with `"a" | (string & {})`.
    // - no-base-to-string: `String(v)` or a template on a value that may be an object prints
    //   `[object Object]`. Narrow the value at its boundary, or pick the text you mean
    //   (`toDisplayText`, `toMessage`, `JSON.stringify` of a JSON value).
    "typescript/no-base-to-string": "error",
    "typescript/no-redundant-type-constituents": "error",
    "typescript/unbound-method": "error",
    // import/no-cycle: a module that reaches itself again through its imports. ESM loads a cycle
